  "cells": [
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "# Topology of $\\mathbb{R}^n$\n\nA topological structure on $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is what lets us talk about \"near\", \"boundary\", \"boundedness\", and \"completeness\" in higher dimensions. The metric\n\n$$d(x, y) = \\|x - y\\|_2 = \\sqrt{\\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i - y_i)^2}$$\n\ngenerates the **standard topology**. Everything else is built on top of this.\n\nThis notebook walks the basic objects:\n\n- **Open ball** $B_r(x_0) = \\{x : d(x, x_0) < r\\}$ — the analogue of an open interval.\n- **Open set** $U \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n$: every point has an open ball entirely inside $U$.\n- **Closed set**: complement of an open set; equivalently, contains all its limit points.\n- **Compact**: closed AND bounded (Heine-Borel — only equivalent like this in finite dimensions).\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "load(\"numerics\")$\nload(\"ax-plots\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 29,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Open balls in $\\mathbb{R}^2$\n\nA 2D open ball is just an open disc. Visualise $B_1(0, 0)$.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "theta_grid : np_to_list(np_linspace(0, 2*float(%pi), 200))$\ndisc_x : map(lambda([t], cos(t)), theta_grid)$\ndisc_y : map(lambda([t], sin(t)), theta_grid)$\n\nax_draw2d(\n  color=\"blue\", line_width=2, name=\"∂B₁(0) — boundary not in B₁\",\n  lines(disc_x, disc_y),\n  marker_size=4, color=\"lightblue\", name=\"interior\",\n  points(np_to_list(np_scale(0.7, ndarray(disc_x))),\n         np_to_list(np_scale(0.7, ndarray(disc_y)))),\n  title=\"Open ball B_1(0) in ℝ² — disc, boundary excluded\",\n  xlabel=\"x₁\", ylabel=\"x₂\",\n  aspect_ratio=true, grid=true, showlegend=true\n)$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 30,
      "outputs": [
        {
