    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Open vs closed: the boundary changes the type\n\nA set $S$ is **closed** iff its complement is open, equivalently iff every convergent sequence in $S$ has its limit in $S$.\n\n- The closed disc $\\overline{B_1(0)} = \\{x : \\|x\\| \\le 1\\}$ is closed (it contains its boundary).\n- $B_1(0)$ is open (it doesn't).\n- $\\{x : \\|x\\| = 1\\}$ alone (just the circle) is closed.\n- $\\{x : 0 < \\|x\\| < 1\\}$ (the *punctured* open disc) is open — removing a point from an open set leaves it open.\n- $\\{x : 0 < \\|x\\| \\le 1\\}$ (puncture the *closed* disc instead) is **neither** open nor closed: it isn't open because no ball around a boundary point stays inside, and it isn't closed because the sequence $x_k = (1/k, 0, \\ldots)$ lies in it but its limit $0$ does not.\n\nA set can be *neither* open nor closed — most subsets of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ are like that.\n\n## Compactness — Heine-Borel in $\\mathbb{R}^n$\n\nIn **finite-dimensional** spaces, $K \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n$ is compact iff $K$ is **closed and bounded**. (In infinite dimensions this fails — closed bounded sets need not be compact.)\n\nCompactness is the property that makes most theorems work:\n\n- Continuous functions on compact sets attain their max and min.\n- Continuous functions on compact sets are uniformly continuous.\n- Sequences in compact sets have convergent subsequences (Bolzano-Weierstrass).\n\nVisualise some compact and non-compact sets in $\\mathbb{R}^2$:\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* The closed unit square (compact) */\nsq_x : [-1, 1, 1, -1, -1]$\nsq_y : [-1, -1, 1, 1, -1]$\n\n/* A bounded but not closed set: the open unit ball, indicated by dashed boundary */\n/* And an unbounded but closed set: the half-plane y >= 0 */\n\nax_draw2d(\n  color=\"green\", line_width=2, name=\"closed square: compact\",\n  lines(sq_x, sq_y),\n  color=\"red\", dash=\"dash\", line_width=2, name=\"open disc: bounded but not closed\",\n  lines(map(lambda([t], 0.6*cos(t)), theta_grid),\n        map(lambda([t], 0.6*sin(t)), theta_grid)),\n  color=\"orange\", line_width=2, name=\"ray (unbounded but closed)\",\n  points([2, 5], [2, 2]),\n  title=\"Compactness in ℝ² — closed AND bounded\",\n  xlabel=\"x₁\", ylabel=\"x₂\",\n  grid=true, aspect_ratio=true, showlegend=true\n)$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 31,
      "outputs": [
        {
