    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "Each ball is contained in some scalar multiple of any other — that's all \"topologically equivalent\" requires. Continuity, compactness, completeness are insensitive to which norm you pick.\n\nIn **infinite-dimensional** spaces (function spaces) this fails — different norms produce genuinely different topologies. That's why functional analysis has so many distinct flavours: $L^1, L^2, L^\\infty, C^0, C^k, $ Sobolev spaces, ...\n\n## Connectedness\n\nA set $S$ is **connected** if it can't be split into two disjoint non-empty subsets that are open *in $S$* (its subspace topology). The relativisation matters: $[0,1]$ is connected, yet it is the union of the disjoint sets $[0, 1/2]$ and $(1/2, 1]$ — neither of which is open in $\\mathbb{R}$. In $\\mathbb{R}^n$ for \"nice\" sets, this is equivalent to **path-connectedness**: any two points can be joined by a continuous path.\n\nExamples in $\\mathbb{R}^2$:\n- A disc is connected.\n- Two disjoint discs together are not connected — two components.\n- The complement of a single point in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ is *still* connected (you can go around the missing point). In $\\mathbb{R}^1$, removing a point splits the line — connectedness depends on the dimension.\n\n## Why this all matters\n\nThe next two notebooks build directly on this:\n\n- **Continuity** in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ requires the open-set definition (or ε-δ in any equivalent norm).\n- **Differentiation** in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ depends on having an open neighbourhood to define the limit on.\n- **Integration** over a region needs the region to be (at least) measurable, with a well-defined boundary.\n\nWithout the topology, none of the rest happens.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Reference\n\nTao, T. (2016) *Analysis II*. 3rd edn. Singapore: Springer Singapore. doi:[10.1007/978-981-10-1804-6](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-981-10-1804-6) — ch. 1, *Metric spaces*.\n\nZorich, V.A. (2016) *Mathematical Analysis II*. 2nd edn. Berlin, Heidelberg: Springer Berlin Heidelberg. doi:[10.1007/978-3-662-48993-2](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-662-48993-2) — ch. 9, *Continuous Mappings (General Theory)* — §9.1 metric spaces, §9.2 topological spaces.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    }
  ]
